The UPy‑POSS self‑healing coating (Wang et al., 2018) is applied to all exterior surfaces. This coating combines POSS nanoparticles for atomic oxygen resistance—forming a protective SiO₂ layer upon AO exposure—with UPy motifs that provide hydrogen‑bond‑mediated self‑healing. Unlike brittle DLC, UPy‑POSS is mechanically flexible, withstands the −60 °C to +40 °C LEO thermal cycle without cracking, and heals any surface damage autonomously.

The ExoTerra Halo Hall‑effect thruster was selected for electric propulsion. It has a flight‑qualified mass of 0.65–0.83 kg, operates at 100–450 W, produces 4–33 mN of thrust, and achieves specific impulse of 700–1,500 s on xenon. Using a conservative mid‑range I_{sp} = 800 s with 0.30 kg xenon on a 12.5 kg wet tile:

\Delta v = I_{sp} \cdot g_0 \cdot \ln\left(\frac{m_0}{m_f}\right) = 800 \times 9.81 \times \ln\left(\frac{12.5}{12.2}\right) \approx 191 \text{ m/s}.

The originally allocated 80 m/s for station‑keeping is therefore achievable with significant margin.

