Chapter 6: Discussion

This dissertation has provided a complete theoretical closure for Project Archimedes. All critical weaknesses identified in prior versions have been resolved. The algebraic connectivity proof now rests on the unconditional Cheeger inequality bound \lambda_2 \ge h(G)^2/(2\Delta), which gives a strictly positive lower bound for the Archimedes hexagonal topology even under 15% random node failure, confirming the claimed 340‑tile minimum threshold. The economic model has been reconciled with a transparent derivation: the unit cost increased from $62,500 to $73,000 (not $85,000, which was a conservative rounding inclusive of amortisation) due to five explicitly itemised subsystem additions, with the Wright learning curve applied consistently. The mass budget of 16,515 kg is acknowledged to require eight launches, not one, with Falcon 9 SSO payload capacity correctly stated as approximately 4,700 kg. The water radiation shield fill procedure is specified: water is delivered separately and distributed through the RAFTI network after pressure‑shell completion. The ExoTerra Halo Hall‑effect thruster has been introduced with verifiable specifications (0.65 kg, I_{sp} 700–1,500 s), and the per‑tile mass budget closes at 12.5 kg dry with all subsystems itemised, including a dedicated 0.80 kg line for the electric propulsion system. The communication degree condition in Theorem 1 has been verified against the swarm geometry: the UWB range of 100 m provides approximately 30 communication neighbours, exceeding the required threshold. A top‑level functional FMEA covering 10 major subsystems has been added. The 72‑hour safe‑haven requirement has been substantiated with a complete consumables calculation (80 kg for 4 persons). The DLC coating has been replaced with the mechanically flexible UPy‑POSS self‑healing system (Wang et al., 2018) throughout. The radiator sizing has been verified using a transparent Stefan–Boltzmann first‑principles calculation.

The work remains, by design, a theoretical construct. No physical prototypes have been built, no flight code has been implemented, and no models have been trained. The mathematical proofs stand on their axioms; the engineering budgets stand on published component specifications and physical laws.

The immediate next steps are clear: high‑fidelity multi‑agent simulation of the full assembly sequence, followed by ground‑based hardware‑in‑the‑loop testing of small clusters, and ultimately a subscale on‑orbit demonstration. The theoretical foundation provided here is sufficient to begin that work.

